\begin{abstract}
Containerized workloads mix stateful databases and stateless services with fundamentally different failure semantics, yet most remediation frameworks apply the same recovery policy to both. Such uniformity overlooks the distinct safety requirements of stateful and stateless workloads. We present a dual-track control plane that separates stateless and stateful workloads into independent eBPF-driven remediation pipelines. Stateless workloads are managed using a confidence-based decision DAG, whereas stateful workloads incorporate a finite-state machine (FSM), shadow execution, and a WebAssembly (WASM) policy layer to enforce safe action sequencing before irreversible recovery operations. We evaluated the framework using 9,132 execution traces and 90 controlled fault-injection experiments across PostgreSQL, Redis, MySQL, and Nginx container workloads. The stateful divergence rate of 1.93\% confirms that accumulated FSM state materially changes dispatch outcomes compared to a memoryless baseline. Mean time to checkpoint dispatch ranged from 10.40 to 11.54\,s; WASM policy evaluation added 27\,$\mu$s and shadow execution 8\,$\mu$s per cycle---both negligible within the 5\,s budget. Workload-aware control plane separation is both feasible and measurably necessary for safe, explainable autonomous remediation in heterogeneous container environments.
\end{abstract}

